  "slug": "trapping-rain-water",
  "title": "Trapping Rain Water",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "category": "Two pointers",
  "patterns": [
    "two pointers"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "precomputing leftMax and rightMax arrays gets you to O(n) time, but costs O(n) space. do you really need the FULL arrays, or just enough information at each step?",
    "if you're standing at the shorter of your two pointers, do you already know enough to compute the water trapped there, even without knowing the exact max on the far side?",
    "keep a running leftMax and rightMax as you move two pointers inward; whichever side is currently shorter, that side's running max is guaranteed to be the limiting height, because something taller must exist further along the other side",
    "move the pointer on the shorter side inward, adding trapped water as you go, since that side's answer is now fully determined"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "using only leftMax without also tracking rightMax, or updating the max at the wrong point in the loop (before vs after computing trapped water)",
    "not recognizing which side is 'safe' to advance, and mistakenly assuming the taller side's water amount is already known",
    "off-by-one errors including or excluding the current bar's own height when computing water above it"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a list of bar `height`s forming an elevation map, one unit wide each. After it rains, water gets trapped between the bars.\n\n**Return the total amount of water trapped.**\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= height.length <= 2 * 10^4",
      "0 <= height[i] <= 10^5"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "strictly increasing or strictly decreasing heights (no water trapped)",
      "a single bar or two bars (no water trapped)",
      "a deep well between two tall bars"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
